<section xml:id="sec-multiplication-of-a-vector-by-a-scalar" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Multiplication of a Vector by a Scalar</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.2 Vectors
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Multiply vectors by scalars
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Multiplying vectors by a scalar.
         Suggested activity: Learners draw a vector and then 2a, 3a and −a on squared paper.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Vector a with a slider for k; ka stretches, shrinks and reverses as k changes. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Essential 2.2 (d)
         Activity: as planning notes (a, 2a, 3a and -a on squared paper) | changed: one half of a and a mistake to explain added
         JSXGraph: fig-scalar-multiple-arrow (assets/jsxgraph/scalar-multiple-arrow.js), as planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/11-vectors/05-multiplication-of-a-vector-by-a-scalar/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> <m>k\mathbf{a}</m> multiplies each component by <m>k</m>; positive <m>k</m> keeps the direction and negative <m>k</m> reverses it; <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>k\mathbf{a}</m> are parallel.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find <m>k\mathbf{a}</m> and combinations such as <m>2\mathbf{a} + 3\mathbf{b}</m>; decide whether a vector is a multiple of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and find <m>k</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> a velocity at twice the speed in the same direction, or the same speed in the opposite direction.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners draw multiples of a vector and see it stretch, shrink and reverse.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Give out squared paper. After part (c), ask: <q>What does the minus sign do to the arrow?</q></p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-stretching-an-arrow">
        <title>Stretching an Arrow</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Let <m>\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Draw <m>\mathbf{a}</m>, <m>2\mathbf{a} = \mathbf{a} + \mathbf{a}</m>, <m>3\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>-\mathbf{a}</m> from different starting points. Write the column of each.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>2\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>3\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>-\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Compare the length and the direction of each arrow with <m>\mathbf{a}</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>2\mathbf{a}</m> is twice as long in the same direction; <m>3\mathbf{a}</m> is three times as long; <m>-\mathbf{a}</m> is the same length in the opposite direction.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Draw <m>\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{a}</m> and write its column.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\begin{pmatrix} 0.5 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>: half as long, same direction.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Juma writes <m>3\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}</m>. What did he do wrong?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>He added <m>3</m> to each component instead of multiplying: <m>3\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <figure xml:id="fig-multiples-of-a">
        <caption>The vector <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and the multiples <m>2\mathbf{a}</m>, <m>-\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{a}</m>.</caption>
        <image width="55%">
            <shortdescription>Vector a = (1, 2) and the vectors 2a, −a and ½a drawn on a grid: 2a twice as long, −a reversed, ½a half as long.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw[very thin, gray!40] (0,0) grid (10,5);
\draw[very thick, -{Stealth}] (1,1) -- node[left] {$\mathbf{a}$} (2,3);
\draw[very thick, -{Stealth}] (3,0.5) -- node[left] {$2\mathbf{a}$} (5,4.5);
\draw[very thick, -{Stealth}] (7,3) -- node[right] {$-\mathbf{a}$} (6,1);
\draw[very thick, -{Stealth}] (8.5,2) -- node[right] {$\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{a}$} (9,3);
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise multiplying a vector by a scalar and parallel vectors.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (a) shows that <m>2\mathbf{a} = \mathbf{a} + \mathbf{a}</m> doubles each component; part (d) is the mistake to avoid.</p></li>
                <li><p>Multiply every component by the scalar.</p></li>
                <li><p>The sign of the scalar gives the direction; all multiples of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> are parallel to <m>\mathbf{a}</m>.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-multiplying-by-a-scalar">
        <title>Multiplying by a Scalar</title>
        <p>
            Here a <term>scalar</term> is just a number. To multiply a vector by a scalar <m>k</m>, multiply every component by <m>k</m>:
            <md>k\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} kx \\ ky \end{pmatrix}</md>
            A scalar multiplies every component; it never adds to them.
        </p>
        <p>
            If <m>k > 0</m>, <m>k\mathbf{a}</m> points the same way as <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and is <m>k</m> times as long. If <m>k \lt 0</m>, <m>k\mathbf{a}</m> points the opposite way and is <m>|k|</m> times as long. In particular <m>-\mathbf{a}</m> is the reverse of <m>\mathbf{a}</m>. Vectors that are multiples of each other are <term>parallel</term>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Combinations such as <m>2\mathbf{a} + 3\mathbf{b}</m> are found by multiplying first and then adding. Subtracting a vector is adding its reverse: <m>\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b} = \mathbf{a} + (-1)\mathbf{b}</m>.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-scalar-multiple-arrow">
        <caption>Drag the tip of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and move the <m>k</m> slider. The thick dashed arrow <m>k\mathbf{a}</m> stretches, shrinks and reverses along the line of <m>\mathbf{a}</m>.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-scalar-multiple" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="9:10" source="jsxgraph/scalar-multiple-arrow.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-scalar-multiple" surface="jsxboard" aspect="9:10"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Set <alert>k</alert> to <m>2</m> and then to <m>3</m>. How do the columns change?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Set <alert>k</alert> to <m>-1</m>. What is the same about <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>-\mathbf{a}</m>, and what is different?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Set <alert>k</alert> to <m>0.5</m>, then drag the tip of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> to <m>(2, -2)</m>. Is <m>0.5\mathbf{a}</m> still parallel to <m>\mathbf{a}</m>?</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-multiples-of-a-vector">
        <title>Multiples of a Vector</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Find <m>3\mathbf{a}</m>, <m>-\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{a}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Multiply every component:</p>
            <p><md>3\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} -12 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix}, \quad -\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -6 \end{pmatrix}, \quad \tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</md></p>
            <p><m>3\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{a}</m> point the same way as <m>\mathbf{a}</m>; <m>-\mathbf{a}</m> points the opposite way.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-combining-two-vectors">
        <title>Combining Two Vectors</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>\mathbf{p} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\mathbf{q} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Find <m>2\mathbf{p} + 3\mathbf{q}</m> and <m>\mathbf{p} - 2\mathbf{q}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p><md>
                <mrow>2\mathbf{p} + 3\mathbf{q} \amp = \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 10 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} -6 \\ 9 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 19 \end{pmatrix}</mrow>
                <mrow>\mathbf{p} - 2\mathbf{q} \amp = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 - (-4) \\ 5 - 6 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}</mrow>
            </md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-is-it-a-multiple">
        <title>Is It a Multiple of a?</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Which of these are multiples of <m>\mathbf{a}</m>? For each multiple, give <m>k</m>.</p>
            <p>(a) <m>\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -9 \end{pmatrix}</m> (b) <m>\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}</m> (c) <m>\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}</m> (d) <m>\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 1.5 \end{pmatrix}</m> (e) <m>\begin{pmatrix} 10 \\ -15 \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Divide each component by the matching component of <m>\mathbf{a}</m>. A multiple gives the same number both times.</p>
            <p>(a) <m>6 \div 2 = 3</m> and <m>-9 \div (-3) = 3</m>: <m>3\mathbf{a}</m>, same direction.</p>
            <p>(b) <m>-4 \div 2 = -2</m> and <m>6 \div (-3) = -2</m>: <m>-2\mathbf{a}</m>, opposite direction.</p>
            <p>(c) <m>4 \div 2 = 2</m> but <m>-5 \div (-3) = \tfrac{5}{3}</m>: not equal, so not a multiple of <m>\mathbf{a}</m>.</p>
            <p>(d) <m>-1 \div 2 = -\tfrac{1}{2}</m> and <m>1.5 \div (-3) = -\tfrac{1}{2}</m>: <m>-\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{a}</m>, opposite direction.</p>
            <p>(e) <m>10 \div 2 = 5</m> and <m>-15 \div (-3) = 5</m>: <m>5\mathbf{a}</m>, same direction.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Type one number in each box of a column vector: the top box is the move across, the bottom box the move up. Use a minus sign for left or down, for example <c>-3</c>.</p>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-multiplying-vectors-by-scalars">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-multiplying-vectors-by-scalars" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/11-vectors/05-multiplication-of-a-vector-by-a-scalar/Multiplying-vectors-by-scalars.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-same-direction-opposite-or-not-parallel">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-same-direction-opposite-or-not-parallel" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/11-vectors/05-multiplication-of-a-vector-by-a-scalar/Same-direction-opposite-or-not-parallel.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        <em>Vectors in everyday life:</em> a weather forecast may say the wind will blow from the same direction tomorrow but twice as strongly. Which scalar multiplies today's wind velocity?
    </p>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-multiplication-by-a-scalar">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-multiply-a-column">
            <statement><p><m>\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Find <m>4\mathbf{a}</m>, <m>-2\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{a}</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\begin{pmatrix} 24 \\ -8 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-combine-p-and-q">
            <statement><p><m>\mathbf{p} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\mathbf{q} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Find <m>3\mathbf{p} + \mathbf{q}</m>, <m>2\mathbf{p} - \mathbf{q}</m> and <m>\mathbf{p} + 2\mathbf{q}</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 10 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 10 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-which-are-multiples">
            <statement><p><m>\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 12 \\ -8 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Which of these are multiples of <m>\mathbf{a}</m>? Give <m>k</m> for each multiple: <m>\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{pmatrix} 24 \\ -16 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{pmatrix} 15 \\ -11 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\ -6 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>k = -\tfrac{1}{2}</m>; <m>k = 2</m>; not a multiple (<m>15 \div 12 = 1.25</m> but <m>-11 \div (-8) = 1.375</m>); <m>k = \tfrac{3}{4}</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-cyclist-velocity">
            <statement><p>A cyclist rides with velocity <m>\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m> m/s (east and north parts). (a) What is her velocity if she rides twice as fast in the same direction? (b) What is her velocity if she rides back the opposite way at the first speed?</p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}</m> m/s (b) <m>\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}</m> m/s</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-find-the-scalar-k">
            <statement><p><m>\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\ 20 \end{pmatrix} = k\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Find <m>k</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>k = -4</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-two-unknown-scalars">
            <title>Challenge: Two Unknown Scalars</title>
            <statement><p>Find <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> such that <m>m\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + n\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Compare the top numbers, then the bottom numbers.</p></hint>
            <answer><p><m>m = 2</m>, <m>n = 3</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Top: <m>2m + n = 7</m>. Bottom: <m>m + 3n = 11</m>. From the first, <m>n = 7 - 2m</m>; then <m>m + 21 - 6m = 11</m>, so <m>m = 2</m> and <m>n = 3</m>. Check: <m>\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 9 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-multiplication-by-a-scalar">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a <q>multiply each box</q> card. Focus on activity parts (a) to (c) and <xref ref="example-multiples-of-a-vector"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-multiply-a-column"/> and <xref ref="ex-cyclist-velocity"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-multiply-a-column"/> to <xref ref="ex-find-the-scalar-k"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-two-unknown-scalars"/>. Extension: write <m>\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m> as a combination of <m>\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m>. (Answer: <m>m = \tfrac{23}{5}</m>, <m>n = -\tfrac{6}{5}</m>; not every answer is a whole number.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

